<!-- Blog -->
<section class="section" id="blog">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>Parenting Blog</h2>
    <p>Stories, insights & reflections on the parenting journey</p>
    <div class="divider"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="blog-grid">
    <div class="blog-card" id="blogcard1">
      <div class="blog-card-header" onclick="toggleBlog('blogfull1', 'blogcard1')">
        <div class="blog-icon">☂️</div>
        <h3>Under One Umbrella: A Single Mother's Brave Love Story</h3>
        <span class="blog-tag">Parenting Story</span>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-card-body" onclick="toggleBlog('blogfull1', 'blogcard1')">
        <p>When Meera became a single mother, the world didn't end in one loud crash. It unraveled quietly, like a thread slipping from a sweater. A touching story about strength, love, and the unbreakable bond between a mother and daughter...</p>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-card-footer" onclick="toggleBlog('blogfull1', 'blogcard1')">
        <span class="author">✍️ Coach Preeti Sachdeva</span>
        <a class="read-more" id="readmore1">Read Full Story ▼</a>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-full" id="blogfull1">
        <div class="blog-body">
          <p>When Meera became a single mother, the world didn't end in one loud crash. It unraveled quietly, like a thread slipping from a sweater. One day she was a wife with a shared plan; the next, she was a mother with a five-year-old daughter, a small rented apartment, and a thousand unspoken fears.</p>
          <p>Her daughter, Tara, didn't fully understand what had changed. She only knew that her mother hugged her a little longer at night and smiled a little harder in the mornings.</p>
          <p>Meera learned quickly that strength was not about never breaking—it was about waking up every day and choosing to keep going for someone else.</p>
          <p>Their mornings began before sunrise. Meera would pack Tara's lunch while reheating last night's tea. Sometimes the electricity would go out, and she'd finish getting Tara ready in dim light, humming songs to keep the mood bright. Tara loved those mornings. She didn't see the exhaustion in her mother's eyes; she only saw the warmth in her voice.</p>
          <div class="dialogue">"Why do you always sing, Mama?" Tara once asked, tying her shoelaces with serious concentration.</div>
          <div class="dialogue">"So the day starts with music," Meera replied, kneeling to fix the uneven bow. "And because you like it."</div>
          <div class="dialogue">Tara grinned. "I do."</div>
          <p>After dropping Tara at school, Meera rushed to her job at a small coaching center. She taught children who struggled with reading and confidence. It was work that paid just enough to survive, but it also gave her purpose. She understood what it meant to feel lost, and she wanted every child to feel seen.</p>
          <p>Evenings were their favorite time. No matter how tired Meera was, she created small rituals for them. Sometimes it was a walk to the park with a shared ice cream. Sometimes it was storytelling on the bed, where Tara demanded a new story every night.</p>
          <div class="dialogue">"Tell me about the brave princess," Tara would say.</div>
          <div class="dialogue">Meera would smile. "This princess lives in a tiny house. She works very hard. And she has the most precious treasure—a daughter who makes her brave."</div>
          <div class="dialogue">Tara would giggle. "That's you and me!"</div>
          <div class="dialogue">"Yes," Meera would whisper. "That's us."</div>
          <p>But there were hard nights too. Nights when bills piled up and sleep didn't come. Nights when Tara asked questions that had no easy answers.</p>
          <div class="dialogue">"Why doesn't Papa live with us?" she asked one night, her voice small.</div>
          <p>Meera's heart tightened. She pulled Tara close.</p>
          <div class="dialogue">"Sometimes adults cannot stay together, but both of us love you very much. And we will always take care of you."</div>
          <div class="dialogue">Tara nodded slowly. "I have you. That's enough."</div>
          <p>Those words stayed with Meera. On the days she felt she wasn't enough—when she couldn't buy the fancy school project materials or missed a school event because of work—she remembered that, to Tara, she was everything.</p>
          <p>One rainy afternoon, Tara came home with a drawing. It showed a tall figure holding a smaller hand under a big umbrella. Above them was a bright sun peeking through clouds.</p>
          <div class="dialogue">"This is us," Tara said proudly. "You're the umbrella. You keep me safe."</div>
          <p>Meera blinked back tears. She had worried so much about what Tara might be missing. She hadn't realized how much her daughter felt protected, loved, and secure.</p>
          <p>Years passed, and their tiny apartment filled with memories—birthday cakes baked from scratch, late-night homework sessions, and quiet talks about dreams. Meera worked hard to give Tara opportunities she never had. Tara, in return, grew into a kind, confident girl who saw her mother not as someone incomplete, but as someone incredibly strong.</p>
          <p>On Tara's tenth birthday, she handed Meera a folded note.</p>
          <div class="dialogue">"Thank you for being my home," it read.</div>
          <p>Meera hugged her tightly. She realized then that being a single mother wasn't about doing everything perfectly. It was about showing up, again and again, with love. It was about turning ordinary days into safe spaces and hard moments into lessons in resilience.</p>
          <p>Their life was not easy. It was not always fair. But it was full—full of laughter, shared struggles, and a bond that grew stronger with every challenge.</p>
          <p>Meera looked at her daughter that night as she slept peacefully, and she felt a quiet pride. She had not just raised a child. Together, they had built a world where love was enough, courage was learned daily, and even in the smallest home, two hearts could create something unbreakable.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="blog-quote">"Sometimes the strongest families are made of just two hearts."</div>
        <div class="blog-author">— Coach Preeti Sachdeva | Parenting Coach</div>
        <button class="blog-collapse-btn" onclick="toggleBlog('blogfull1', 'blogcard1')">Close ▲</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="blog-card" id="blogcard2">
      <div class="blog-card-header" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg, #2E7D5B 0%, #4CAF82 100%);" onclick="toggleBlog('blogfull2', 'blogcard2')">
        <div class="blog-icon">🌱</div>
        <h3>The 7 Pillars of Child Development Every Parent Must Know</h3>
        <span class="blog-tag">Child Development</span>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-card-body" onclick="toggleBlog('blogfull2', 'blogcard2')">
        <p>Every child is a seed waiting to bloom. But growth doesn't happen by accident — it needs the right soil, sunlight, and care. Here are the 7 pillars that shape your child's development...</p>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-card-footer" onclick="toggleBlog('blogfull2', 'blogcard2')">
        <span class="author">✍️ Coach Preeti Sachdeva</span>
        <a class="read-more" id="readmore2">Read Full Article ▼</a>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-full" id="blogfull2">
        <div class="blog-body">
          <p>Every child is a seed waiting to bloom. But growth doesn't happen by accident — it needs the right soil, sunlight, and care. As parents, we often focus only on academics, forgetting that a child's development is like a tree with many branches. If we nurture only one branch, the tree grows lopsided.</p>
          <p>In my 15 years of working with children and parents, I've seen one truth again and again: <strong>the children who thrive are not the ones pushed the hardest — they are the ones supported the wisest.</strong></p>
          <p>Here are the <strong>7 pillars of child development</strong> that every parent should understand:</p>
          <p><strong>1. Physical Development 🏃‍♂️</strong></p>
          <p>This is more than just height and weight. It includes fine motor skills (holding a pencil, buttoning a shirt) and gross motor skills (running, jumping, climbing). Encourage outdoor play every single day. A child who moves freely thinks freely. Limit screen time and replace it with playground time — their body and brain will thank you.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Cognitive Development 🧠</strong></p>
          <p>This is how your child thinks, learns, and solves problems. Asking open-ended questions like "What do you think will happen if...?" sparks curiosity. Puzzles, building blocks, and even cooking together build cognitive muscles. Remember — a child learns more from exploring than from memorizing.</p>
          <p><strong>3. Emotional Development ❤️</strong></p>
          <p>Can your child name what they feel? Emotional intelligence starts early. When a child says "I'm angry," don't say "Don't be angry." Instead, say "I see you're angry. Tell me what happened." Validating emotions teaches children that feelings are safe to express. This one skill shapes their relationships for life.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Social Development 🤝</strong></p>
          <p>Sharing, taking turns, making friends, resolving conflicts — these are skills, not instincts. Children learn social behavior by watching us. If we shout to solve problems, they learn to shout. If we apologize when wrong, they learn humility. Group activities, team sports, and even family dinners are classrooms for social growth.</p>
          <p><strong>5. Language & Communication 🗣️</strong></p>
          <p>Talk to your child — not at them. Read stories together every night. Ask them to describe their day. A child with a rich vocabulary is a child with a powerful tool for self-expression. Don't rush to correct grammar; instead, model the right way naturally.</p>
          <p><strong>6. Creative Development 🎨</strong></p>
          <p>Creativity is not just art — it's problem-solving, imagination, and original thinking. Give your child open-ended materials: crayons, clay, cardboard boxes, fabric. Let them create without instructions. When a child draws a purple sun, don't say "The sun is yellow." Say "Tell me about your sun!" You'll be amazed at what they imagine.</p>
          <p><strong>7. Moral & Value Development 🌟</strong></p>
          <p>Kindness, honesty, respect, empathy — these are caught, not taught. Children absorb values from their environment. Tell them stories of courage and compassion. Involve them in small acts of kindness — feeding a stray animal, helping a neighbor, sharing with a friend. These tiny moments build character that lasts a lifetime.</p>
          <p style="margin-top:25px;"><strong>The Takeaway for Parents</strong></p>
          <p>You don't need to be a perfect parent. You need to be a <strong>present</strong> one. Watch your child. Listen to them. Play with them. Every interaction is an opportunity to nurture one of these pillars.</p>
          <p>And remember — development is not a race. Every child blooms at their own pace. Your job is not to rush the flower, but to water it with love, patience, and understanding.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="blog-quote">"A child's mind is not a vessel to be filled, but a fire to be kindled." — Plutarch</div>
        <div class="blog-author">— Coach Preeti Sachdeva | Parenting With Preeti</div>
        <button class="blog-collapse-btn" onclick="toggleBlog('blogfull2', 'blogcard2')">Close ▲</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


